<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <meta name="description" content="Read a complete fictional Oriental Destiny case report updated to match the latest BaZi engine structure, including Four Pillars, hidden stems, ten gods, seasonal command, timing, and practical support.">
    <meta property="og:title" content="Case Report | Oriental Destiny">
    <meta property="og:description" content="A complete fictional BaZi case report aligned with the latest Oriental Destiny reading model.">
    <meta property="og:type" content="website">
    <title>Case Report | Oriental Destiny</title>
    <link rel="icon" href="favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
    <style>
        @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Cormorant+Garamond:wght@400;600;700&display=swap');

        :root {
            --bg-paper: #f7f4ee;
            --ink: #1f1a17;
            --cinnabar: #a92b20;
            --gold: #ad8650;
            --line: #ddd4c7;
            --soft: #faf7f1;
            --soft-gold: #f3ecde;
        }

        * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }

        body {
            font-family: "Cormorant Garamond", Georgia, serif;
            background: var(--bg-paper);
            color: var(--ink);
            line-height: 1.86;
            font-size: 11.5pt;
        }

        .page {
            width: 210mm;
            min-height: 297mm;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 22mm 18mm;
            background: #fff;
            box-shadow: 0 0 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.08);
            page-break-after: always;
            position: relative;
        }

        @media print {
            body { background: #fff; }
            .page { box-shadow: none; margin: 0; padding: 18mm 15mm; }
            .no-print { display: none; }
        }

        h1 { font-size: 31pt; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 10pt; }
        h2 { font-size: 18pt; font-weight: 600; margin-top: 22pt; margin-bottom: 10pt; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--gold); padding-bottom: 5pt; }
        h3 { font-size: 13.5pt; font-weight: 600; margin-top: 14pt; margin-bottom: 7pt; }
        p { margin-bottom: 10pt; text-align: justify; }

        .cover {
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            text-align: center;
            min-height: 253mm;
        }

        .seal-img {
            width: 100px;
            height: 100px;
            object-fit: contain;
            margin-bottom: 28px;
        }

        .subtitle { font-size: 14pt; font-style: italic; color: #555; margin-bottom: 24px; }
        .meta { font-size: 11.2pt; color: #444; line-height: 2; }
        .master-line { margin-top: 40px; font-size: 12pt; color: var(--cinnabar); font-weight: 600; }
        .disclaimer { font-size: 9pt; color: #777; margin-top: 46px; max-width: 82%; }

        .section-header {
            font-size: 10pt;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            letter-spacing: 3px;
            color: var(--gold);
            margin-bottom: 4pt;
        }

        .chapter-title {
            font-size: 20pt;
            font-weight: 700;
            color: var(--cinnabar);
            margin-bottom: 14pt;
        }

        .handwritten-box,
        .master-note,
        .jewelry-box,
        .table-wrap,
        .note-box,
        .timing-box,
        .compat-card {
            background: var(--soft);
            border: 1px solid var(--line);
            padding: 14pt 16pt;
            margin: 14pt 0;
        }

        .handwritten-box { border-left: 3px solid var(--gold); }
        .master-note { border-left: 3px solid var(--cinnabar); }
        .jewelry-box { background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--soft-gold) 0%, #fcfaf5 100%); }

        .master-note-header {
            font-size: 11pt;
            font-weight: 600;
            color: var(--cinnabar);
            margin-bottom: 8pt;
        }

        .pillars-table,
        .detail-table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin: 14pt 0;
            font-size: 10.5pt;
        }

        .pillars-table th,
        .detail-table th {
            background: var(--ink);
            color: #fff;
            padding: 7pt 8pt;
            font-weight: 600;
            text-align: left;
        }

        .pillars-table td,
        .detail-table td {
            padding: 7pt 8pt;
            border: 1px solid var(--line);
            vertical-align: top;
        }

        .grid-2 {
            display: grid;
            grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
            gap: 12pt;
            margin: 12pt 0;
        }

        .compat-card h3,
        .timing-box h3,
        .note-box h3 {
            margin-top: 0;
            color: var(--cinnabar);
        }

        .mini-list {
            list-style: none;
            display: grid;
            gap: 8pt;
            margin: 0;
        }

        .mini-list li {
            padding-left: 12pt;
            position: relative;
        }

        .mini-list li::before {
            content: "";
            width: 5px;
            height: 5px;
            border-radius: 50%;
            background: var(--gold);
            position: absolute;
            left: 0;
            top: 11px;
        }

        .date-row {
            display: flex;
            justify-content: space-between;
            gap: 16px;
            padding: 6pt 0;
            border-bottom: 1px solid #eee5d8;
        }

        .date-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
        .date-label { font-weight: 600; }
        .date-desc { color: #666; text-align: right; }

        .signature-block { margin-top: 38pt; text-align: right; }
        .signature-name { font-size: 14pt; font-weight: 600; }
        .signature-title { font-size: 11pt; color: #666; margin-top: 4pt; }
        .red-ink { color: var(--cinnabar); font-weight: 600; }
        .emphasis { font-weight: 600; }

        .print-btn {
            position: fixed;
            bottom: 30px;
            right: 30px;
            background: var(--cinnabar);
            color: #fff;
            border: none;
            padding: 14px 28px;
            font-size: 14pt;
            font-family: inherit;
            border-radius: 30px;
            cursor: pointer;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 12px rgba(169, 43, 32, 0.28);
        }

        @media (max-width: 900px) {
            .page {
                width: 100%;
                min-height: auto;
                padding: 32px 18px;
            }

            .grid-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
            .date-row { flex-direction: column; }
            .date-desc { text-align: left; }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <button class="print-btn no-print" onclick="window.print()">Print / Save as PDF</button>

    <div class="page cover">
        <img class="seal-img" src="assets/seal.jpg" alt="Official Seal">
        <h1>Personal Destiny Case Report</h1>
        <div class="subtitle">A complete BaZi reading presented in the Oriental Destiny tradition</div>
        <div class="meta">
            <strong>Prepared for:</strong> Evelyn Zhao<br>
            <strong>Date of Birth:</strong> March 14, 1988 at 9:36 PM<br>
            <strong>Birth Location:</strong> Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada<br>
            <strong>Primary Focus:</strong> Wealth, retention, and the keeping of resources<br>
            <strong>Report Date:</strong> April 29, 2026
        </div>
        <div class="master-line">Read in the Oriental Destiny manner</div>
        <div class="disclaimer">
            This case report uses the profile of a fictional person and is presented as a model reading. It is offered for self-reflection, symbolic guidance, and product illustration only. It is not medical, legal, or financial advice.
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-header">From the Reader</div>
        <h2>Opening Counsel</h2>
        <p>
            Evelyn's chart begins with <span class="red-ink">Bing Fire</span> as the Day Master — born in <span class="red-ink">Spring</span>, carrying a <span class="red-ink">Balanced</span> strength. That is the first thing to hold: this is not a chart that lacks fire. It has enough warmth, enough presence, enough ability to move and attract. What it needs is not more spark. It needs better containment of what the spark creates.
        </p>
        <p>
            The deeper reading reveals a chart rich in Wood and Earth, and notably thin in Metal. Opportunity, movement, and the capacity to generate are not the problem here. The problem is what happens after — how quickly energy disperses, how little gets held, and how the person can create far more than they retain. The central wealth question is not abundance. It is custody.
        </p>

        <div class="master-note">
            <div class="master-note-header">Senior Reader's Note</div>
            <p>
                Wood leads this chart. Metal is the thinnest function present. Earth sits as the strongest secondary. The reading therefore begins with a simple directive: <strong>the first correction is not urgency. The first correction is order.</strong>
            </p>
        </div>

        <h3>The First Meaning of the Chart</h3>
        <p>
            Bing Fire as Day Master gives a person who is expressive, quick to respond, naturally visible, and able to draw attention. In this chart, that brightness is real — but it is also the very thing that scatters. The person generates opportunity easily. They attract interest, they start things, they move in multiple directions at once. The harder question is what stays. What gets kept. What accumulates into something durable rather than flowing through and away.
        </p>

        <div class="handwritten-box">
            <strong>In plain terms:</strong> this is a person who can create, attract, and move opportunity — but who benefits most when wealth is slowed, contained, documented, and turned into something that stays.
        </div>

        <div class="signature-block">
            <div class="signature-name">Your Oriental Destiny Senior Reader</div>
            <div class="signature-title">Longhu Mountain Lineage</div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-header">Chart Structure</div>
        <div class="chapter-title">Four Pillars and the Main Architecture</div>

        <table class="pillars-table">
            <tr>
                <th>Year Pillar</th>
                <th>Month Pillar</th>
                <th>Day Pillar</th>
                <th>Hour Pillar</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Wu Chen / Earth Dragon</td>
                <td>Yi Mao / Wood Rabbit</td>
                <td>Bing Yin / Fire Tiger</td>
                <td>Ji Hai / Earth Pig</td>
            </tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            The Year Pillar places Earth over Dragon, giving the ancestral field a practical and productive tone. The Month Pillar places Wood over Rabbit, which strengthens growth, planning, responsiveness, and the seasonal gate of the chart. The Day Pillar roots the self in Bing Fire over Tiger, while the Hour Pillar closes the chart with Earth over Pig — adding a second Earth stem to the private ambition and later-development layer.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="detail-table">
                <tr>
                    <th>Pillar</th>
                    <th>Field of Life</th>
                    <th>Stem-Branch Pattern</th>
                    <th>How It Reads</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Year Pillar</strong></td>
                    <td>Ancestral field, early environment, distant networks</td>
                    <td>Wu Earth — Shi Shen (Food God)</td>
                    <td>Productivity and practical output are present from the start. The person learns early that skill produces value.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Month Pillar</strong></td>
                    <td>Career field, adult responsibility, strongest seasonal gate</td>
                    <td>Yi Wood — Zheng Guan (Direct Officer)</td>
                    <td>The chart benefits from method, learning, and repeatable structure. Systems and competence matter more than improvisation.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Day Pillar</strong></td>
                    <td>Self, partner palace, intimate pattern</td>
                    <td>Bing Fire — Bi Jian (Outgoing Letter) over Xin Yin (Skill)</td>
                    <td>The self is warm and expressive, but thinks in layers. Choices are shaped by instinct, insight, and a need to keep inner reserves intact.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Hour Pillar</strong></td>
                    <td>Private ambition, later life, legacy</td>
                    <td>Ji Earth — Shang Guan (Direct Audit) over Wu Guan (Seven Killings)</td>
                    <td>Later life carries edge: the capacity to challenge limits and turn pressure into action. Without structure, this same layer scatters energy.</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="note-box">
            <h3>The Main Dynamic</h3>
            <p>
                The chart is well-supplied with Wood and Earth, but lacks the clean Metal function that cuts, sorts, and holds. The money question is therefore not about earning power — it is about refinement, retention, and disciplined handling. The person who can generate is already present. The person who can keep is not yet fully developed.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="page">
        <div class="section-header">Hidden Structure</div>
        <div class="chapter-title">Hidden Stems, Elements, and Ten Gods</div>

        <p>
            Beyond the visible stems sits a second layer — the hidden stems — where the chart stores its quieter motivations, its concealed support, and the roles that only surface under pressure or over time.
        </p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="detail-table">
                <tr>
                    <th>Branch</th>
                    <th>Hidden Stems</th>
                    <th>Reading</th>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Year Branch — Chen</strong></td>
                    <td>Wu Earth, Yi Wood, Gui Water</td>
                    <td>Earth carries the Shi Shen (Food God) function here. Below it, Wood as Zheng Guan and Water as Zheng Guan complete the picture: productive output sits at the surface, but learning and outer expectation are also stored inside the field. The person is already trained to produce.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Month Branch — mao</strong></td>
                    <td>Yi Wood</td>
                    <td>The seasonal gate is clean and singular: Zheng Guan. This supports study, planning, refinement, and growth through method rather than force. It is the strongest structural ally in the chart.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Day Branch — Yin</strong></td>
                    <td>Jia Wood, Bing Fire, Wu Earth</td>
                    <td>The self sits on Jia as Jia (Renewal), Bing as Xin Yin (Skill), and Wu as Shi Shen (Food God). This three-layer stem structure is why the chart can keep generating motion and openings — there is always something beneath the surface pushing forward.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Hour Branch — Hai</strong></td>
                    <td>Ren Water, Jia Wood</td>
                    <td>The private and future-facing layer stores Wu Guan (Seven Killings) and Xin Yin (Skill). Pressure and instinct both live here. Ambition sharpens as life progresses, but so does the risk of overextension if the Earth-Metal structure is not in place.</td>
                </tr>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h3>Five Elements Diagnosis</h3>
        <table class="detail-table">
            <tr>
                <th>Element</th>
                <th>Engine Weight</th>
                <th>Status</th>
                <th>Meaning in This Chart</th>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong class="red-ink">Wood</strong></td>
                <td>7.0</td>
                <td><span class="red-ink">High</span></td>
                <td>Growth, planning, responsiveness, and movement are abundant. The person generates direction quickly. But excess Wood also keeps money circulating — opportunity arrives and leaves just as fast.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong class="red-ink">Earth</strong></td>
                <td>6.5</td>
                <td><span class="red-ink">High</span></td>
                <td>Earth is the chart's primary remedy: containment, routine, management, and practical follow-through. It is why the person functions well when grounded, and why habits and structure matter more than inspiration.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Fire</strong></td>
                <td>2.5</td>
                <td>Low</td>
                <td>Fire is present through the Day Master but not dominant in the full spread. The capacity to shine and attract is real, but it must not outrun structure. Without containment, brightness burns briefly.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Water</strong></td>
                <td>2.5</td>
                <td>Low</td>
                <td>Water sharpens instinct and timing. In moderation it helps. In excess it weakens retention. It is not the primary medicine here — its role is secondary at best.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Metal</strong></td>
                <td>0.0</td>
                <td>Thin</td>
                <td>Metal is the clearest missing function in the chart. It governs sorting, boundaries, discernment, accounting, and the discipline that keeps gain from leaking away. This is the element that must be built — gently, and through Earth habit rather than force.</td>
            </tr>
